              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for a nymph, then the Latin for a fleece."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ōrāculum, ōrāculī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōrāculum, ōrāculī — \"an oracle\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Lēgātī ad ōrāculum missī sunt. Envoys were sent to the oracle."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Neuter, from ōrō, 'to speak, to pray' (taught). Both the place where a god answered and the answer itself."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ōrāculum is built on ōrō, \"to speak, to pray\", with the ending -culum (Lewis & Short). English oracle comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
