          "title": "You'll want to know: prōdigium, prōdigiī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōdigium, prōdigiī — \"a portent, a wonder\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Lapidibus pluit; hoc prōdigium omnēs terruit. It rained stones; this portent frightened everyone."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Neuter. Roman historians list the prōdigia of each year: a rain of stones, a cow that spoke. English prodigy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "prōdigium is explained by Lewis & Short as standing for prōdicium, from prōdīcō, \"to foretell\": a sign that speaks of what will come. English prodigy comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
